<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bernardo Tanucci</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1698">1698</a>–<a href="/years/1783">1783</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bernardo Tanucci (1698–1783) was a Tuscan jurist who became the leading minister of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles of Bourbon</a> and regent during the minority of Ferdinand IV. A champion of royal authority, he curbed the privileges of the church and the barons, restricted the jurisdiction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> in 1767. He lost power in 1776 through the influence of Queen Maria Carolina.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bernardo-tanucci.jpg" alt="Bernardo Tanucci" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicola Matraini · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicola_Matraini,_ritratto_di_Bernardo_Tanucci,_1738_(Pisa,_Palazzo_alla_Giornata)_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
